3) REAL LIFE ANALOGY — NORMALIZED DATA
===============================================================================

Think of school records.

Instead of repeating class teacher name in every student record,
we store teacher once and reference by teacher_id.

This avoids duplication.

That is NORMALIZATION.

WHY it exists:
Reduce redundancy + prevent inconsistent updates. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

===============================================================================
4) NORMALIZATION (SUPER IMPORTANT)
===============================================================================

WHY normalization:
Repeated data creates update bugs.

Bad design:
Order table stores user address in every row.

If user changes address:
1000 rows update required.

Normalized design:
USERS table stores address once
ORDERS references user_id

Benefits:
- storage efficient
- single source of truth
- easier updates
- referential integrity
- no update anomalies
- great for OLTP correctness

Real example:
Payment systems:
customer KYC must stay correct globally.

Tradeoff:
Reads may require joins.

This is the classic SQL read tradeoff.

