6) RELATIONAL DATABASE DOWNSIDES
===============================================================================

Jordan’s downside section is VERY interview-relevant.

A) JOINS ACROSS MANY NODES ARE SLOW
-----------------------------------
WHY:
When horizontally partitioned, related rows may live on different shards.

Now one query needs:
network fanout + merge + distributed sort.

Example:
Instagram feed joining:
- posts
- likes
- comments
- follows
across shards

This becomes expensive. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

B) TWO-PHASE COMMIT IS SLOW
-----------------------------------
If one transaction touches multiple shards:
need distributed transaction coordination.

Flow:
1. prepare all shards
2. vote
3. commit

Adds:
- network RTT
- WAL sync
- coordinator blocking
- lock holding

Very expensive at scale. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

C) HORIZONTAL SCALING HARDER
-----------------------------------
Strong ACID + joins + referential integrity
make shard boundaries difficult.

D) SCHEMA EVOLUTION COST
-----------------------------------
Frequent product iteration can make migrations painful.

===============================================================================
7) WHY NON-RELATIONAL (NoSQL) EXISTS
===============================================================================

WHY:
Internet-scale systems needed:
- flexible schema
- billions of rows
- easy sharding
- predictable key access
- geo replication
- high write throughput

Social feed, logs, events, sessions, cache, counters
fit poorly into strict normalized joins.

So NoSQL emerged.

Golden line:
“NoSQL optimizes around access pattern and scale, not relational purity.” :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}

